From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 06:32:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] egl: let a host release the memory its screens cache

Zink keeps the buffers its clients freed in a cache, and the slab
backing behind small ones, until a client frees enough or they expire.
The embedding host charges every allocation of the process to one pool,
and what these caches hold counts against it. An allocation that another
screen's cache could have satisfied was refused all the same, because
only the screen that was itself refused cleaned up before trying again:
Zink's own retry cannot see a Venus guest's allocation, which is made on
a Vulkan device of the same process that Zink never touches.

EGL_MESA_context_trim gains eglReleaseCachedMemoryMESA(), found through
eglGetProcAddress.  It frees what every screen of the process caches and
nothing uses, and returns how many cached allocations that released.  It
takes no display, no context and no current thread: it is called by the
allocator that was refused, which may hold a display's lock, so a
display lock would deadlock it.  The DRI frontend keeps a locked list of
the screens that can release, which a screen leaves before it is
destroyed.

pipe_screen::release_cached_memory is the driver's side. Zink releases
its slabs and its buffer cache, the pair its allocator already empties
when an allocation fails, under the same locks and on any thread.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
